## 8️⃣ Test Way 8: DOM Storage & Local Credential Leakage
Many modern applications copy fallback session validation keys, backup parameters, or sensitive profile settings directly into browser DOM storage locations that survive simple cookie deletions.
## 📋 Step-by-Step Execution

   1. Open your browser tab, authenticate your account, and hit F12 to toggle the Developer Console.
   2. Go to the Application or Storage pane tab on your console.
   3. Click through the drop-down listings for Local Storage and Session Storage. Note down every custom key/value pair (e.g., user_profile_cache, refresh_token, or cached_balance).
   4. Click the website Logout button natively.
   5. Do not close or refresh the window. Immediately review the Local Storage table values again to check which attributes were actively cleaned.

## 🔍 Response Verification Criteria

* Vulnerable Outcome ($750 - $1,000 Information Disclosure Path): Highly confidential fields (such as identity details or active tokens used to query authorization profiles) remain inside the browser's persistent DOM registry after the UI signals a successful logout event. An attacker at a public internet café or shared computer can read your Local Storage variables directly out of the machine to extract your data or re-authenticate your account.

